{
  "dom_id": "step-q49049",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/c924791712d00c951d9718fa976a00f35b2b3deb35d83301431ddb55d22326de.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{2x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x)=2x+4{e}^{4x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
